<!-- What Is Insulated Drylining -->
<h2 style="font-size:1.75rem;font-weight:700;color:var(--secondary);margin:2.5rem 0 1rem;">What Is Insulated Drylining?</h2>
<p style="font-size:1.0625rem;color:var(--text);line-height:1.8;margin-bottom:1.5rem;">Insulated drylining is a dual-purpose construction system that provides both a smooth internal wall finish and effective thermal insulation in a single installation. Unlike traditional wet plastering, insulated drylining creates a thermal barrier between the internal space and the external wall, significantly reducing heat loss and improving comfort.</p>
<p style="font-size:1.0625rem;color:var(--text);line-height:1.8;margin-bottom:1.5rem;">There are two main approaches to insulated drylining. The first uses insulated plasterboard, where a rigid insulation layer is bonded directly to the back of the plasterboard. The second involves installing insulation within a timber or metal stud frame before fixing plasterboard to the frame. Both methods are effective, and the best choice depends on your property type, wall construction, and performance requirements.</p>
<p style="font-size:1.0625rem;color:var(--text);line-height:1.8;margin-bottom:1.5rem;">For Edinburgh's vast stock of Victorian and Georgian sandstone tenements (over 300,000 tonnes of sandstone make up the city's iconic architecture), insulated drylining is one of the most effective ways to improve thermal performance without altering the external appearance of the building — a particular advantage in conservation areas across the New Town, Old Town, and Stockbridge.</p>

