<!-- FAQ -->
<h2 style="font-size:1.75rem;font-weight:700;color:var(--secondary);margin:2.5rem 0 1rem;">Frequently Asked Questions about Insulated Drylining</h2>
<div>
  <div style="padding:20px;margin-bottom:12px;background:var(--white);border-radius:var(--radius);border:1px solid var(--border);">
    <h3 style="font-size:16px;margin-bottom:8px;color:var(--primary);">What is insulated drylining?</h3>
    <p style="font-size:14px;color:var(--text-light);margin:0;">Insulated drylining combines plasterboard with thermal insulation in a single installation system. This can be achieved using insulated plasterboard (which has a rigid insulation layer bonded to the back of the board) or by placing insulation within a timber or metal stud frame behind the plasterboard.</p>
  </div>
  <div style="padding:20px;margin-bottom:12px;background:var(--white);border-radius:var(--radius);border:1px solid var(--border);">
    <h3 style="font-size:16px;margin-bottom:8px;color:var(--primary);">How much can insulated drylining improve home efficiency?</h3>
    <p style="font-size:14px;color:var(--text-light);margin:0;">Insulated drylining can significantly reduce heat loss through external walls, making rooms warmer in winter and cooler in summer. The exact improvement depends on the insulation type, thickness, and the existing construction, but most homes see noticeable improvements in comfort and heating costs.</p>
  </div>
  <div style="padding:20px;margin-bottom:12px;background:var(--white);border-radius:var(--radius);border:1px solid var(--border);">
    <h3 style="font-size:16px;margin-bottom:8px;color:var(--primary);">What insulation materials do you use?</h3>
    <p style="font-size:14px;color:var(--text-light);margin:0;">We use PIR insulation boards from Celotex and Kingspan, mineral wool batts from Rockwool and Isover, and insulated plasterboard combining plasterboard with rigid insulation. The best choice depends on your wall type, available space, and performance requirements.</p>
  </div>
  <div style="padding:20px;margin-bottom:12px;background:var(--white);border-radius:var(--radius);border:1px solid var(--border);">
    <h3 style="font-size:16px;margin-bottom:8px;color:var(--primary);">Is insulated drylining suitable for Edinburgh's older properties?</h3>
    <p style="font-size:14px;color:var(--text-light);margin:0;">Yes, insulated drylining is highly suitable for Edinburgh's vast stock of Victorian, Edwardian, and Georgian sandstone tenements. However, careful consideration of moisture movement and ventilation is essential in these solid wall properties. We always recommend a professional assessment before installation to ensure the right system is specified.</p>
  </div>
  <div style="padding:20px;margin-bottom:12px;background:var(--white);border-radius:var(--radius);border:1px solid var(--border);">
    <h3 style="font-size:16px;margin-bottom:8px;color:var(--primary);">How much internal space is lost with insulated drylining?</h3>
    <p style="font-size:14px;color:var(--text-light);margin:0;">Insulated drylining typically adds 50 to 120 mm to each treated wall, depending on the insulation thickness required. Insulated plasterboard is the most space-efficient option, while framed systems with separate insulation take up slightly more space.</p>
  </div>
  <div style="padding:20px;margin-bottom:12px;background:var(--white);border-radius:var(--radius);border:1px solid var(--border);">
